                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_09\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit removes the MOUSE_TRANSPARENT property-based save/restore mechanism in TransitionHandler.java, replacing:\n  previousNode.setMouseTransparent((Boolean) previousNode.getProperties().get(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT));\nwith:\n  view.setMouseTransparent(false);\nand replacing:\n  previousNode.getProperties().put(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT, previousNode.isMouseTransparent());\n  previousNode.setMouseTransparent(true);\nwith:\n  view.setMouseTransparent(true);\nIt also deletes the MOUSE_TRANSPARENT constant entirely. The fix commit message states \"Set mouseTransparent of the whole view, fix bug\" \u2014 indicating the bug was that mouse transparency was only being toggled on individual nodes (previousNode) instead of the whole container view, which could leave the view in an inconsistent mouse-interactable state during/after transitions.\n\ncandidate_09 is the commit that first introduces this exact buggy pattern: it adds the MOUSE_TRANSPARENT constant, adds the properties-based save of previousNode.isMouseTransparent(), and sets previousNode.setMouseTransparent(true) instead of applying it to the whole view. This is precisely the code structure the fix later removes.\n\ncandidate_10 subsequently modifies TransitionHandler.java but only wraps the same buggy block inside Platform.runLater() without changing the underlying mouseTransparent logic \u2014 it inherits the bug rather than introducing it. candidate_11's diff is identical to the fix_commit_diff itself (same hunks, same before/after hashes cec4a9d4..b350bb9a), meaning it is the fix, not the bug's origin. No candidate prior to candidate_09 contains any MOUSE_TRANSPARENT or related setMouseTransparent logic, confirming candidate_09 is where the buggy behavior was first introduced.\n"
